{"id": "aic-47417", "status": "ok", "meta": "A snake crawls over a memorial altar in Hokusai's Memorial Anniversary, from the One Hundred Ghost Tales series, about 1831.", "about": "A snake crawls over the things set out for a memorial service: a box of sweets, a spirit tablet with the dead person's Buddhist name, and a green leaf floating in a bowl of water. The museum says the snake stands for malevolence. Hokusai seems to have planned a hundred ghost tales, but only five prints are known. The title comes from a night game of telling scary stories, snuffing out a candle after each one. The bright blue is Berlin blue, newly cheap then, and the museum says viewers of the time would have linked it with death.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/47417", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "24.3 × 18.5 cm (9 5/8 × 7 5/16 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/47417", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-47417"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "aic-65709", "status": "ok", "meta": "Whistler holds palette and brush among Japanese scrolls and Chinese porcelain in The Artist in His Studio, painted in the mid-1860s.", "about": "Whistler looks out at us with palette and brush, surrounded by his own collection. Three Japanese scrolls hang on the wall and Chinese porcelain fills shelves on the left. The museum says he used thin layers of muted paint to flatten the picture like a Japanese woodblock print. It also sees a link to Velázquez, who put himself at his easel in Las Meninas. By the mid-1860s Whistler was an American in London, and the museum thinks he placed himself at the center of joining Western and Eastern art.", "museum_record": "https://www.artic.edu/artworks/65709", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "Board: 62 × 46.5 cm (24 7/16 × 18 5/16 in.); Overall: 63 × 47.3 cm (24 13/16 × 18 5/8 in.)", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.artic.edu/artworks/65709", "https://artnap.com/artwork/aic-65709"], "check": "clean"}
